Solution A attempts a classic "bounce-back" marketing tactic, but the critiques reveal it to be an operational liability. By relying on manual receipt modification and physical tallying, it creates significant bottlenecks during peak hours, introduces high risks of fraud, and lacks the digital oversight required for accurate ROI tracking. While theoretically clever, it essentially trades labor and potential revenue leakage for marginal retention gains.

Solution B pivots toward gamification to build customer rapport. While it is more creative and avoids the messy paperwork of Solution A, it suffers from the same fundamental operational flaws: it relies on high-touch staff interactions in environments that prioritize high-velocity service. It is slightly more feasible because it requires less physical management than tracking thousands of receipts, but it is ultimately hindered by the "cringe" factor and the inconsistency of human-led games during stressful rush periods.

